.SH FUNCTIONS
.SS has_colors
.B \%has_colors
returns
.B TRUE
if the terminal supports colors and
.B FALSE
if it does not.
\fB\%initscr\fP(3X) or \fB\%newterm\fP(3X) must be called first,
but
.B \%start_color
need not be.
An application might call
.B \%has_colors
to inform its decision whether to use color
or a video attribute like
.B \%A_BOLD
to render text.
.SS start_color
If your application requires color,
call
.B \%start_color
before any other color manipulation function.
As a rule,
do so immediately after
.BR \%initscr "."
If the terminal type supports color,
.BR \%start_color ":"
.bP
initializes the two global variables,
.B \%COLORS
and
.BR \%COLOR_PAIRS ","
described above;
.bP
initializes (only) the color pair
.B 0
to the terminal type's power-up foreground and background colors
(but see the
.I \%ncurses
extension \fB\%use_default_colors\fP(3X));
.bP
initializes the color palette;
and
.bP
selects color pair
.BR 0 "."
.\" ...by calling internal functions `set_{fore,back}ground_color()`.
.PP
.B \%start_color
sets up the color palette
for the eight colors named by the X/Open Curses standard
(see section \*(``CONSTANTS\*('' above)
applying weights appropriate to the color space.
.I curses
does not attempt to initialize the color palette
to match a terminal type's power-up configuration.
.\" Init and reset string capabilities could do so.
See section \*(``NOTES\*('' below.
.PP
Calling
.B \%start_color
again after it has returned
.B OK
does nothing.
.SS init_pair
(Re-)define a color pair with
.BR \%init_pair ","
which takes three arguments:
the color pair identifier to be updated,
a foreground color index,
and
a background color index.
A portable application restricts these argument values
to the valid ranges stated above.
If the application uses
.IR \%ncurses 's
default color extension
(see below),
the library adjusts the upper limit to allow for extra pairs
that use a default color in the foreground and/or background.
.PP
If a color pair was previously defined,
.B \%init_pair
causes a refresh of the entire screen,
and all occurrences of that color pair change to use its new definition.
.I \%ncurses
suppresses this refresh if the color pair's new color indices
are the same as the old.
.PP
.I \%ncurses
extensions
allow you to update color pair
.B 0
via \fB\%assume_default_colors\fP(3X),
and to access the terminal's default colors as color index
.B \-1
if you first call \fB\%use_default_colors\fP(3X).
.SS init_extended_pair
Because
.B \%init_pair
uses signed
.IR short s
for its parameters,
its color pair identifiers and color indices
are limited to 32767 even for terminal types that are much more capable.
.\" ...not 65535 because the System V terminfo compiled entry format
.\" foreclosed use of an unsigned type for this purpose and others.
This
.I \%ncurses
extension uses
.IR int s
instead,
expanding their range.
.SS pair_content
An application can discover the color index assignments of a color pair
with
.BR \%pair_content "."
Its first argument is the color pair identifier of interest,
and the remaining two are each a pointer to
.I short
that the function populates
with the foreground and background color indices,
respectively.
.SS extended_pair_content
Because
.B \%pair_content
uses signed
.IR short s
for its parameters,
its color pair identifiers and color indices
are limited to 32767 even for terminal types that are much more capable.
.\" ...not 65535 because the System V terminfo compiled entry format
.\" foreclosed use of an unsigned type for this purpose and others.
This
.I \%ncurses
extension uses
.IR int s
instead,
expanding their range.
.SS reset_color_pairs
This
.I \%ncurses
extension directs the library to discard
all color pair assignments configured by application calls of
.B \%init_pair
and
.BR \%init_extended_pair "."
.\" Due to the library's internal design, this has the effect of
.\" resetting color pair zero, so it still exists and works afterward.
It furthermore marks the entire screen as requiring refresh;
an application can thus easily reconfigure its color scheme
by subsequently initializing as many color pairs as required.
.SS can_change_color
.B \%can_change_color
returns
.B TRUE
if the terminal supports colors and can change their mappings,
and
.B FALSE
if it does not.
.SS init_color
Change a color's mapping (definition) by supplying this function
four arguments:
the color index;
and
red,
green,
and
blue
color channel values in the range 0-1000.
.PP
If the color index is in use in a color pair on the screen,
all occurrences of it change to use its new definition.
No \fB\%refresh\fP(3X) is necessary.
.\" The library essentially does `putp(tiparm("initc", idx, r, g, b));`.
.\" But for the interposition of the higher-level color palette ncurses
.\" stores in `SCREEN`, `init_color` would be a "lower-level" API, like
.\" `mvcur()`.
.SS init_extended_color
Because
.B \%init_color
uses signed
.IR short s
for its parameters,
the maximum value of its color index argument
is limited to 32767 even for terminal types that are much more capable.
(The range of valid RGB channel values remains 0-1000.)
.\" Here the System V terminfo compiled entry format imposes no
.\" limitation, because these values exist only as parameters to string
.\" capabilities.  We still say "32767" because that is the smallest
.\" possible maximum value of a `signed short` in standard C.
This
.I \%ncurses
extension uses
.IR int s
instead,
expanding the range of permissible color indices.
.PP
If the color index is in use in a color pair on the screen,
all occurrences of it change to use its new definition.
No \fB\%refresh\fP(3X) is necessary.
.\" The library essentially does `putp(tiparm("initc", idx, r, g, b));`.
.\" But for the interposition of the higher-level color palette ncurses
.\" stores in `SCREEN`, `init_extended_color` would be a "lower-level"
.\" API, like `mvcur()`.
.SS color_content
An application can query a color index's location in RGB space
by calling
.BR \%color_content .
The library stores the color channel values
corresponding to the specified color index
in the
.IR red ","
.IR green ","
and
.I blue
.RI pointer-to- short
arguments.
.SS extended_color_content
Because
.B \%color_content
uses signed
.IR short s
for its parameters,
the maximum value of its color index argument
is limited to 32767 even for terminal types that are much more capable.
This
.I \%ncurses
extension uses
.IR int s
instead,
expanding the range of permissible color indices.
